1
00:00:00,615 --> 00:00:04,790
Sometime in the mid nineteen-nineties, a system administrator

2
00:00:04,640 --> 00:00:07,080
at a university got a phone call he

3
00:00:06,930 --> 00:00:09,330
would never forget.

4
00:00:09,220 --> 00:00:11,950
The head of the statistics department was on

5
00:00:11,800 --> 00:00:14,470
the line, and he had a strange complaint.

6
00:00:15,500 --> 00:00:18,730
"We can't send email more than five hundred

7
00:00:18,580 --> 00:00:19,700
miles."

8
00:00:19,800 --> 00:00:21,760
Not "email is broken."

9
00:00:21,800 --> 00:00:24,070
Not "the server is down."

10
00:00:24,180 --> 00:00:28,650
Email worked perfectly, to anywhere within about five

11
00:00:28,775 --> 00:00:29,230
hundred miles.

12
00:00:29,660 --> 00:00:32,810
Beyond that, it simply failed.

13
00:00:32,820 --> 00:00:34,640
That sounds impossible.

14
00:00:34,640 --> 00:00:37,220
Email doesn't care about distance...

15
00:00:37,230 --> 00:00:38,130
does it?

16
00:00:38,080 --> 00:00:40,050
But the department was right.

17
00:00:40,270 --> 00:00:42,350
And the reason turned out to be one

18
00:00:42,200 --> 00:00:45,770
of the strangest bugs in computing history.

19
00:00:45,620 --> 00:00:48,920
A bug caused not by bad code, but

20
00:00:48,770 --> 00:00:50,994
by the speed of light itself.

21
00:00:51,900 --> 00:00:54,830
The sysadmin's name was Trey Harris, and he

22
00:00:54,680 --> 00:00:57,620
worked at the University of North Carolina at

23
00:00:57,470 --> 00:00:58,870
Chapel Hill.

24
00:00:59,100 --> 00:01:02,510
His first reaction was probably the same as

25
00:01:02,360 --> 00:01:05,210
yours: that's not how email works.

26
00:01:05,340 --> 00:01:08,570
Email isn't a paper plane that runs out

27
00:01:08,420 --> 00:01:09,250
of fuel.

28
00:01:09,340 --> 00:01:12,040
Once a message leaves your computer, it travels

29
00:01:11,890 --> 00:01:14,850
through cables and routers, and it doesn't really

30
00:01:14,700 --> 00:01:18,220
matter whether it's going across the street or

31
00:01:18,070 --> 00:01:19,770
across the planet.

32
00:01:19,700 --> 00:01:22,840
So he asked a simple question: how do

33
00:01:22,690 --> 00:01:25,550
you know it's five hundred miles?

34
00:01:25,500 --> 00:01:27,510
And this is where it gets good.

35
00:01:27,500 --> 00:01:29,670
Remember, these were statisticians.

36
00:01:30,040 --> 00:01:32,510
They had been watching the problem for a

37
00:01:32,360 --> 00:01:35,770
few days, and instead of just complaining, they

38
00:01:35,620 --> 00:01:36,940
collected data.

39
00:01:36,860 --> 00:01:39,480
They sent test emails to colleagues all over

40
00:01:39,330 --> 00:01:41,730
the country and marked every result on a

41
00:01:41,580 --> 00:01:42,560
map.

42
00:01:42,420 --> 00:01:44,170
Green for delivered.

43
00:01:44,250 --> 00:01:45,690
Red for failed.

44
00:01:45,660 --> 00:01:49,420
When they stepped back, the pattern was impossible

45
00:01:49,270 --> 00:01:50,120
to ignore.

46
00:01:50,220 --> 00:01:53,960
Every success sat inside a circle centered on

47
00:01:53,810 --> 00:01:55,050
Chapel Hill.

48
00:01:55,155 --> 00:01:57,510
Every failure sat outside it.

49
00:01:57,560 --> 00:02:00,490
And the radius of that circle was...

50
00:02:00,340 --> 00:02:03,280
a little over five hundred miles.

51
00:02:03,340 --> 00:02:04,700
Data doesn't lie.

52
00:02:04,640 --> 00:02:07,510
But to understand why that's so strange, we

53
00:02:07,360 --> 00:02:10,230
need to look at what actually happens when

54
00:02:10,080 --> 00:02:10,812
you hit send.

55
00:02:11,780 --> 00:02:14,550
When you send an email, your computer doesn't

56
00:02:14,510 --> 00:02:16,010
deliver it directly.

57
00:02:15,980 --> 00:02:18,600
It hands the message to a mail server,

58
00:02:18,610 --> 00:02:21,750
a computer whose whole job is moving mail.

59
00:02:21,840 --> 00:02:25,720
Back then, on most university systems, that program

60
00:02:25,570 --> 00:02:27,080
was called Sendmail.

61
00:02:27,340 --> 00:02:30,360
Sendmail looks at the address, say, someone at

62
00:02:30,210 --> 00:02:32,130
a university in California.

63
00:02:32,060 --> 00:02:35,170
It asks the internet's phone book, the Domain

64
00:02:35,020 --> 00:02:38,640
Name System, which computer accepts mail for that

65
00:02:38,490 --> 00:02:39,370
university.

66
00:02:39,380 --> 00:02:42,860
Then it does something important: it tries to

67
00:02:42,710 --> 00:02:45,560
open a connection to that computer.

68
00:02:45,540 --> 00:02:48,560
That connection is built using a protocol called

69
00:02:48,410 --> 00:02:50,490
TCP, and it starts with a kind of

70
00:02:50,340 --> 00:02:52,010
digital handshake.

71
00:02:52,020 --> 00:02:54,640
Your server sends a tiny packet that basically

72
00:02:54,490 --> 00:02:56,490
says, "Hello, are you there?"

73
00:02:56,420 --> 00:02:58,900
The other server replies, "Yes, I'm here.

74
00:02:58,820 --> 00:02:59,800
Are you?"

75
00:02:59,820 --> 00:03:01,720
And your server answers, "Yes.

76
00:03:01,785 --> 00:03:02,810
Let's talk."

77
00:03:02,660 --> 00:03:05,780
Only after this handshake does the actual email

78
00:03:05,630 --> 00:03:09,410
get sent: sender, recipient, subject, message.

79
00:03:09,740 --> 00:03:12,280
Now here's the key detail.

80
00:03:12,260 --> 00:03:15,640
Your server can't wait forever for a reply.

81
00:03:15,540 --> 00:03:18,320
The other machine might be switched off, or

82
00:03:18,170 --> 00:03:20,480
broken, or a cable might be cut.

83
00:03:20,420 --> 00:03:23,500
So every mail server has a timer, called

84
00:03:23,350 --> 00:03:24,380
a timeout.

85
00:03:24,360 --> 00:03:26,960
If the "yes, I'm here" doesn't come back

86
00:03:26,810 --> 00:03:29,190
in time, the server gives up and tries

87
00:03:29,040 --> 00:03:30,060
again later.

88
00:03:30,260 --> 00:03:32,640
Normally that timeout is generous.

89
00:03:32,630 --> 00:03:34,850
Seconds, sometimes minutes.

90
00:03:34,700 --> 00:03:37,490
A round trip across the internet usually takes

91
00:03:37,340 --> 00:03:39,600
just a fraction of a second, so with

92
00:03:39,450 --> 00:03:42,700
a timeout of a few seconds, distance never

93
00:03:42,550 --> 00:03:43,470
matters.

94
00:03:43,460 --> 00:03:45,180
Keep that timer in mind.

95
00:03:45,180 --> 00:03:48,129
Because in Chapel Hill, something had happened to

96
00:03:47,979 --> 00:03:48,129
it.

97
00:03:49,339 --> 00:03:53,100
Trey still didn't fully believe the five-hundred-mile story.

98
00:03:53,310 --> 00:03:56,390
So he did what any good engineer does:

99
00:03:56,260 --> 00:03:58,280
he tested it himself.

100
00:03:58,180 --> 00:04:00,700
He logged into the mail server and started

101
00:04:00,550 --> 00:04:03,450
connecting to other mail servers by hand, one

102
00:04:03,300 --> 00:04:04,330
at a time.

103
00:04:04,180 --> 00:04:07,690
A server close by: connected instantly.

104
00:04:07,540 --> 00:04:10,370
A server a couple of hundred miles away:

105
00:04:10,370 --> 00:04:11,360
connected.

106
00:04:11,440 --> 00:04:14,320
Then he tried one much farther away, well

107
00:04:14,170 --> 00:04:15,890
outside the circle.

108
00:04:15,740 --> 00:04:16,820
Nothing.

109
00:04:16,780 --> 00:04:18,610
The connection just failed.

110
00:04:18,610 --> 00:04:20,240
Almost immediately.

111
00:04:20,120 --> 00:04:22,660
He tried another distant server.

112
00:04:22,650 --> 00:04:23,790
Same thing.

113
00:04:23,740 --> 00:04:27,550
Then one just inside the five-hundred-mile mark.

114
00:04:27,610 --> 00:04:28,840
It worked.

115
00:04:28,740 --> 00:04:30,720
The statisticians were right.

116
00:04:30,780 --> 00:04:34,170
Somehow, his mail server could only reach machines

117
00:04:34,020 --> 00:04:36,330
that were physically close to it.

118
00:04:36,340 --> 00:04:38,600
And that's when he remembered something.

119
00:04:38,460 --> 00:04:41,200
A few days earlier, a consultant had installed

120
00:04:41,050 --> 00:04:44,520
a routine update on that server's operating system.

121
00:04:44,540 --> 00:04:45,510
Nothing special.

122
00:04:45,540 --> 00:04:47,760
The kind of thing that happens all the

123
00:04:47,610 --> 00:04:48,160
time.

124
00:04:48,010 --> 00:04:51,750
Except this "routine" update had done something nobody

125
00:04:51,600 --> 00:04:52,022
noticed.

126
00:04:52,900 --> 00:04:57,650
The update replaced Sendmail with a different version.

127
00:04:57,700 --> 00:04:59,110
Not a newer one.

128
00:04:59,100 --> 00:05:00,520
An older one.

129
00:05:00,420 --> 00:05:03,740
The server had been running Sendmail version 8,

130
00:05:03,700 --> 00:05:07,920
and the update quietly swapped in Sendmail version

131
00:05:07,770 --> 00:05:08,900
5.

132
00:05:08,820 --> 00:05:11,940
But the configuration file, the instructions that tell

133
00:05:11,790 --> 00:05:14,930
Sendmail how to behave, was still written for

134
00:05:14,780 --> 00:05:16,320
version 8.

135
00:05:16,260 --> 00:05:19,340
Imagine handing someone a manual written for a

136
00:05:19,190 --> 00:05:20,490
newer machine.

137
00:05:20,380 --> 00:05:23,340
They read it, they hit instructions they don't

138
00:05:23,190 --> 00:05:27,050
understand, and instead of stopping, they just skip

139
00:05:26,900 --> 00:05:27,690
them.

140
00:05:27,700 --> 00:05:29,870
That's exactly what version 5 did.

141
00:05:29,780 --> 00:05:33,120
It ignored every setting it didn't recognize.

142
00:05:33,060 --> 00:05:36,040
And one of the settings it skipped was

143
00:05:35,890 --> 00:05:36,820
the timeout.

144
00:05:36,780 --> 00:05:39,340
With no timeout setting at all, the value

145
00:05:39,190 --> 00:05:40,890
was effectively set to zero.

146
00:05:41,060 --> 00:05:43,650
Zero seconds to wait for a reply.

147
00:05:43,580 --> 00:05:47,270
Now, a computer can't actually do anything in

148
00:05:47,120 --> 00:05:48,050
zero time.

149
00:05:48,180 --> 00:05:51,280
Starting the connection, checking the clock, deciding to

150
00:05:51,130 --> 00:05:53,210
give up: all of that takes a little

151
00:05:53,060 --> 00:05:53,810
while.

152
00:05:53,740 --> 00:05:56,340
On that machine, it worked out to roughly

153
00:05:56,190 --> 00:05:57,200
three milliseconds.

154
00:05:57,670 --> 00:06:00,180
Three thousandths of a second.

155
00:06:00,100 --> 00:06:02,760
So the server would send out its "hello,"

156
00:06:02,910 --> 00:06:06,610
wait about three milliseconds, and then give up.

157
00:06:06,540 --> 00:06:09,140
If the reply arrived in time, the email

158
00:06:08,990 --> 00:06:10,170
went through.

159
00:06:10,100 --> 00:06:12,870
If it didn't, it failed.

160
00:06:12,840 --> 00:06:16,790
Which raises the question: how far can anything

161
00:06:16,640 --> 00:06:17,990
travel in three milliseconds?

162
00:06:19,140 --> 00:06:22,760
Nothing in the universe moves faster than light.

163
00:06:22,660 --> 00:06:26,552
In a vacuum, light travels about three hundred

164
00:06:26,631 --> 00:06:29,050
thousand kilometers every second.

165
00:06:29,060 --> 00:06:32,260
That's fast enough to circle the Earth more

166
00:06:32,110 --> 00:06:34,560
than seven times in one second.

167
00:06:34,580 --> 00:06:38,300
But a millisecond is very short.

168
00:06:38,220 --> 00:06:42,050
In one millisecond, light travels about three hundred

169
00:06:41,900 --> 00:06:44,690
kilometers, roughly a hundred and eighty-six miles.

170
00:06:44,660 --> 00:06:47,040
So Trey opened a terminal and used a

171
00:06:46,890 --> 00:06:50,500
little Unix tool called "units," which converts between

172
00:06:50,350 --> 00:06:52,470
almost any units you like.

173
00:06:52,340 --> 00:06:54,770
He typed: three millilightseconds.

174
00:06:56,550 --> 00:06:58,840
Convert to miles.

175
00:06:58,780 --> 00:07:02,655
And the answer came back: five hundred and

176
00:07:02,638 --> 00:07:04,000
fifty-eight point eight four miles.

177
00:07:04,350 --> 00:07:05,810
Five hundred miles.

178
00:07:05,880 --> 00:07:07,790
Or a little bit more.

179
00:07:08,340 --> 00:07:11,270
The circle on the statisticians' map wasn't a

180
00:07:11,120 --> 00:07:12,350
coincidence.

181
00:07:12,260 --> 00:07:15,000
It was the distance light could travel before

182
00:07:14,850 --> 00:07:16,670
the server gave up waiting.

183
00:07:16,660 --> 00:07:19,900
Any mail server inside that circle could answer

184
00:07:19,750 --> 00:07:20,650
in time.

185
00:07:20,580 --> 00:07:24,680
Anything outside was, physically, too far away to

186
00:07:24,530 --> 00:07:25,470
reply.

187
00:07:25,380 --> 00:07:27,890
The bug didn't just break email.

188
00:07:27,820 --> 00:07:31,230
It turned a software timer into a measuring

189
00:07:31,080 --> 00:07:32,416
stick for the speed of light.

190
00:07:33,190 --> 00:07:35,700
Now, if you're the kind of person who

191
00:07:35,550 --> 00:07:38,340
checks the math, you might be feeling a

192
00:07:38,190 --> 00:07:40,350
little uneasy.

193
00:07:40,300 --> 00:07:41,740
And you'd be right.

194
00:07:41,660 --> 00:07:45,080
First, signals in a network don't travel at

195
00:07:44,930 --> 00:07:46,710
the full speed of light.

196
00:07:46,900 --> 00:07:50,140
Inside glass fiber, light slows down to about

197
00:07:49,990 --> 00:07:52,330
two-thirds of its vacuum speed.

198
00:07:52,180 --> 00:07:55,650
Second, the handshake is a round trip.

199
00:07:55,620 --> 00:07:58,350
The "hello" has to go out, and the

200
00:07:58,200 --> 00:08:00,260
reply has to come back.

201
00:08:00,220 --> 00:08:02,700
So in three milliseconds, a signal in fiber

202
00:08:02,550 --> 00:08:05,796
could really only reach a server around a

203
00:08:05,769 --> 00:08:08,330
hundred and eighty miles away and return.

204
00:08:08,180 --> 00:08:09,932
Not five hundred.

205
00:08:09,860 --> 00:08:12,750
And on top of that, every router along

206
00:08:12,600 --> 00:08:15,070
the way adds its own tiny delay.

207
00:08:15,060 --> 00:08:17,970
So the numbers in the story don't line

208
00:08:17,820 --> 00:08:19,160
up perfectly.

209
00:08:19,100 --> 00:08:21,780
Trey Harris himself has said that he simplified

210
00:08:21,630 --> 00:08:23,540
and rounded details when he wrote it up

211
00:08:23,390 --> 00:08:24,860
years later.

212
00:08:24,900 --> 00:08:27,690
The exact timing was never measured in a

213
00:08:27,540 --> 00:08:28,070
lab.

214
00:08:28,100 --> 00:08:30,690
But the core of the story is real.

215
00:08:30,540 --> 00:08:33,210
A timeout of zero really did turn a

216
00:08:33,060 --> 00:08:35,940
time limit into a distance limit.

217
00:08:35,860 --> 00:08:38,200
And the deeper idea, that the speed of

218
00:08:38,050 --> 00:08:40,240
light puts a hard floor on how fast

219
00:08:40,090 --> 00:08:44,170
information can travel, isn't a story at all.

220
00:08:44,265 --> 00:08:45,530
It's physics.

221
00:08:45,460 --> 00:08:47,980
And it's shaping your internet right now.

222
00:08:48,940 --> 00:08:51,910
Take a trip from Mumbai to New York.

223
00:08:51,900 --> 00:08:54,990
That's about twelve and a half thousand kilometers

224
00:08:54,840 --> 00:08:56,210
in a straight line.

225
00:08:56,300 --> 00:08:58,800
Even if we laid a perfectly straight fiber

226
00:08:58,650 --> 00:09:01,800
cable between them, light inside that glass would

227
00:09:01,650 --> 00:09:04,780
need about sixty milliseconds to get there, and

228
00:09:04,630 --> 00:09:06,700
another sixty to come back.

229
00:09:06,620 --> 00:09:09,350
So no matter how good the technology gets,

230
00:09:09,350 --> 00:09:12,040
a reply from New York can never arrive

231
00:09:11,890 --> 00:09:14,370
in less than about a hundred and twenty-five

232
00:09:14,220 --> 00:09:15,810
milliseconds.

233
00:09:15,820 --> 00:09:18,970
Real cables don't run in straight lines, so

234
00:09:18,820 --> 00:09:22,130
actual ping is usually closer to two hundred.

235
00:09:21,980 --> 00:09:24,440
That's why gamers in India get high ping

236
00:09:24,290 --> 00:09:26,910
on American servers, and why no amount of

237
00:09:26,760 --> 00:09:29,200
faster Wi-Fi can fix it.

238
00:09:29,125 --> 00:09:31,780
It's why Netflix and YouTube keep copies of

239
00:09:31,630 --> 00:09:34,030
videos on servers near your city.

240
00:09:34,050 --> 00:09:36,530
It's why stock traders once spent hundreds of

241
00:09:36,380 --> 00:09:39,910
millions of dollars on straighter cables and microwave

242
00:09:39,760 --> 00:09:43,070
towers between Chicago and New York, just to

243
00:09:42,920 --> 00:09:45,450
shave off a few milliseconds.

244
00:09:45,380 --> 00:09:48,060
And it's why driving a rover on Mars

245
00:09:47,910 --> 00:09:50,180
in real time is impossible.

246
00:09:50,220 --> 00:09:52,730
Depending on where the planets are, a radio

247
00:09:52,580 --> 00:09:55,410
signal takes between about three and twenty-two minutes

248
00:09:55,260 --> 00:09:56,410
to get there.

249
00:09:56,260 --> 00:09:59,550
Engineers can't steer it like a remote-control car.

250
00:09:59,420 --> 00:10:01,602
They send instructions, and wait.

251
00:10:02,540 --> 00:10:05,520
So the next time a page loads slowly

252
00:10:05,370 --> 00:10:09,570
or a video call lags, remember that statistics

253
00:10:09,420 --> 00:10:11,830
department and their map.

254
00:10:11,860 --> 00:10:15,660
Somewhere underneath all our software, there's a speed

255
00:10:15,510 --> 00:10:18,890
limit that no update can change.

256
00:10:18,860 --> 00:10:21,720
Back in Chapel Hill, the fix was simple:

257
00:10:21,700 --> 00:10:24,090
put the right version of Sendmail back, and

258
00:10:23,940 --> 00:10:25,680
the timeout returned to normal.

259
00:10:25,820 --> 00:10:28,820
Emails flew to California again.

260
00:10:28,760 --> 00:10:31,560
But the story stuck around for decades, because

261
00:10:31,540 --> 00:10:34,230
it's a reminder that computers don't live in

262
00:10:34,080 --> 00:10:36,620
some magical digital world.

263
00:10:36,520 --> 00:10:40,220
They live in ours, made of glass, copper,

264
00:10:40,320 --> 00:10:41,420
and light.

265
00:10:41,420 --> 00:10:44,150
This is the first episode of Physics Bugs:

266
00:10:44,220 --> 00:10:46,920
real moments when the laws of the universe

267
00:10:46,770 --> 00:10:48,200
broke our computers.

268
00:10:48,320 --> 00:10:51,690
Next time: the day one extra second crashed

269
00:10:51,540 --> 00:10:53,310
half the internet.

270
00:10:53,220 --> 00:10:54,520
Subscribe so you don't miss it.
